>>>markdown
# Storefront — a guided tour

A small but complete ecommerce model: an `order_items` fact joined to `customers`
and `products`, plus a `regions` lookup DuckDB reads straight from CSV — 25,356
order lines across 11,000 orders and three years. Every number below is composed
from the model's reusable measures, so the metrics stay correct across joins
(Malloy's symmetric aggregates). Open [`storefront.malloy`](storefront.malloy) to
see how they're defined.

The dashboard below is the summary; each section after it takes one question a
level deeper. Almost every cell is a named view from the model, run on its own.

The **Parameters** panel above comes from the four givens the next cell imports:
the same declarations in [`givens.malloy`](givens.malloy) that make up the filter
row on the [dashboards](dashboards/overview), rendered here as a panel. Change one
and every cell re-runs, with the values in the page URL — so a filtered read of
this notebook is a link you can send. `REGION` is declared in that file too and
deliberately left out: a notebook gets a control for every given it imports, so
importing one that nothing here filters by would leave a control that moves
nothing. See [`docs/givens.md`][g].

[g]: https://github.com/malloydata/publisher/blob/main/docs/givens.md
>>>malloy
##! experimental.givens
import "storefront.malloy"
import { CATEGORY, BRAND, SINCE, MIN_SALE } from "givens.malloy"

// One `where:` for the whole document: every query below runs against this
// source, so four controls scope all of it. A dashboard makes the same choice
// inside each query (dashboards/overview.malloy) — what a given filters is
// always the document's decision, never the declaration's.
source: orders_in_scope is order_items extend {
  where:
    category ~ $CATEGORY
    and brand ~ $BRAND
    and created_at >= $SINCE
    and sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE
}
>>>markdown
## Business overview

One `# dashboard` view, one query: four KPI cards, a monthly trend, revenue by
state as a `# shape_map` choropleth, the category split, and the ten products
that earn the most. $2.1M of revenue at a 47% gross margin, over 11,000 orders
averaging $191.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> business_overview
>>>markdown
## Growth, year over year

Revenue grew by about a third in each of the last two years: $503K in 2023, $682K
in 2024, $913K in 2025.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> sales_by_year
>>>markdown
## Seasonality

The same twelve months, one line per year. Two things hold across all three years:
November and December are the peak — 2025 took $237K of its $913K in those two
months — and every month outsells the same month a year earlier. The growth above
is not one good quarter.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> seasonality
>>>markdown
## Where the revenue is

The dashboard's map breaks revenue out by state. This rolls those states up into
sales regions, where the South alone is 37% of revenue, nearly twice the
Northeast's 19%.

The state-to-region mapping is a CSV sitting next to the Parquet in `data/`, and
`duckdb.table()` reads both formats in place, so the lookup joins to the order
data with no conversion step and no connection to set up.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> sales_by_region
>>>markdown
## Category mix

The dashboard ranks categories by revenue. The table below adds what that ranking
hides: Outerwear leads on 22% of revenue but earns one of the thinnest rates in
the catalog, 45.7%, while Shorts sells less than half as much at the best rate,
49.8%. Dresses trails Footwear by $10K of revenue and passes it on margin dollars,
by $339.

The category names in the table are clickable: they take a pointer cursor and read
as links under the pointer, the same way they do on a dashboard. `# drill` is
declared on the model's `category` dimension, not on any one document, so any cell
that groups by it is clickable with no notebook-specific wiring.

Clicking one offers the same two destinations a dashboard tile offers, because
this notebook imports the given the tag names: **Category** opens
[Category Detail](dashboards/category) with that category filled in, and **Filter
this notebook** writes it into the Category control above instead of leaving the
page. The second entry is the one that needs the import — a document with no
control for `CATEGORY` has nowhere to put the value, so the drill is not offered
there rather than dead-ending. See [`docs/dashboards.md`][d].

[d]: https://github.com/malloydata/publisher/blob/main/docs/dashboards.md
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> category_performance
>>>markdown
## Brands

A near dead heat at the top: the four biggest brands are within $2,800 of one
another on about $220K each, so their order in the ranking is noise rather than a
finding. The rate column is where they separate, and it runs the other way — the
best of it belongs to Harbor, tenth by revenue and keeping 50.2 cents of every
dollar, against 43.6 for Willow.

Brand is drillable too, and shows the other shape a drill takes: its tag names one
destination (`# drill { to=self }`), so clicking a brand filters this notebook in
place, with no menu to choose from.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> brand_performance
>>>markdown
## What sells, and what sells a lot

The dashboard's top-products tile ranks by revenue, and it is all outerwear: nine
of those ten are jackets, parkas, or coats north of $200 a line. Ask the same
question by units and almost none of them come back — the volume is in shorts,
four of the top ten, and everyday basics mostly under $60 a line. One product
makes both lists: the Meridian Court Boot, 258 sold at $109.

This is the only cell that isn't a named view. Views are for the questions worth
asking again; a question you're asking once is a query written where you stand,
against the same source and the same measures.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> {
  top: 10
  group_by:
    # label="Product"
    products.name
  aggregate:
    order_item_count
    # currency
    # label="Avg line price"
    avg_line_price is sale_price.avg()
    total_sales
  order_by: order_item_count desc
}
>>>markdown
## Top customers

974 customers placed those 11,000 orders, eleven apiece over three years. The
best of them, Delilah Okafor, spent $8,818 across 43 of them.
>>>malloy
run: orders_in_scope -> top_customers
>>>markdown
Same model, other surfaces: the [dashboards](dashboards/overview) lay these views
out as pages with the filter row across the top, and the package's data app serves
a hand-written HTML page from `public/` that queries the same source. None of the
three redefines anything: the measures, the labels, and the drill tags are written
once, in [`storefront.malloy`](storefront.malloy).
